            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>When to Use This SOP</h2>
<p>This guide is designed for overseas ad operations teams or finance personnel managing multiple advertising accounts. Follow these steps when you need to regularly reconcile recharge amounts, actual spend, and platform-issued invoices across Meta, TikTok Ads, Google Ads, and other platforms.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 1: Gather Original Data</h2>
<blockquote>
<p>Three types of data are essential before starting reconciliation.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>Required Documents</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Data Type</th>
<th>Source</th>
<th>Format</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Recharge Records</td>
<td>Internal finance system or payment receipts</td>
<td>Excel/CSV with date, amount, account ID</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Spend Reports</td>
<td>Export from ad platform dashboards</td>
<td>Periodic spend summary</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Invoice Copies</td>
<td>Finance archive or email attachments</td>
<td>PDF/screenshot</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Key Points</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>Set the reconciliation period</strong>: Monthly alignment is recommended for consistency with invoice cycles.</li>
<li><strong>Label account ownership</strong>: The same entity may have multiple ad accounts with different recharge entry points.</li>
<li><strong>When exporting spend reports, select "confirmed spend only"</strong> to exclude pre-authorized amounts caused by timezone differences.</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>Step 2: Verify Recharge Amounts</h2>
<h3>Standard Process</h3>
<p><strong>Step 1</strong>: Open the recharge record sheet and group by account ID.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2</strong>: Log in to the corresponding ad platform and navigate to "Billing" &gt; "Transactions."</p>
<p><strong>Step 3</strong>: Export platform-side recharge records and compare line by line with internal records.</p>
<p><strong>Step 4</strong>: Flag discrepancies (amount mismatch, time difference over 24 hours).</p>
<h3>Common Causes of Discrepancies</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Currency conversion differences</strong>: Recharges in USD may be invoiced in RMB or EUR. Use the exchange rate on the recharge date as the benchmark.</li>
<li><strong>Fee variations</strong>: Some channels charge a 1%-3% service fee on recharges; verify whether this is included in the total.</li>
<li><strong>Recharge delay</strong>: Certain platforms (e.g., TikTok enterprise accounts) may have a 2-4 hour delay in credit processing.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Ops Tip</strong>: Record both "actual credited amount" and "platform displayed amount" in your internal log for easier future reference.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Step 3: Verify Ad Spend</h2>
<h3>Relationship Between Spend and Invoice</h3>
<p>For most overseas ad platforms, the invoice amount follows this formula:</p>
<pre><code>Invoice Amount ≈ Total Recharge - Account Balance - Refunded Amount
</code></pre>
<h3>Verification Steps</h3>
<ol>
<li>Sum the total spend across all ad accounts for the month.</li>
<li>Compare with "Total Recharge" minus "Current Account Balance."</li>
<li>Confirm the discrepancy falls within an acceptable range (typically under 0.5%).</li>
</ol>
<h3>Investigating Abnormal Spend</h3>
<p>If spend appears unusually high, check:</p>
<ul>
<li>Active legacy campaigns still running</li>
<li>"Accelerated" delivery mode accidentally enabled</li>
<li>Invalid traffic or abnormal clicks (compare "Amount Spent" detail from ad reports)</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Step 4: Request and Verify Invoices</h2>
<h3>Invoice Access Paths</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Platform</th>
<th>Invoice Request Location</th>
<th>Billing Cycle</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Meta Business</td>
<td>Billing &gt; Payments &gt; Request Invoice</td>
<td>After the 5th of each month</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>TikTok Ads</td>
<td>Finance &gt; Invoice Management &gt; Request Invoice</td>
<td>After the 10th of each month</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Google Ads</td>
<td>Tools &gt; Billing &gt; Invoices</td>
<td>After the 3rd of each month</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Verification Checklist</h3>
<p>Upon receiving an invoice, verify:</p>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Company name matches the business license</li>
<li>[ ] Invoice amount matches "actual amount owed" on the platform (not account balance)</li>
<li>[ ] Invoice date falls within the corresponding recharge/spend period</li>
<li>[ ] Tax information (rate, taxpayer ID) is complete</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Step 5: Generate Reconciliation Report</h2>
<p>After completing the above steps, produce a monthly reconciliation report with this template:</p>
<pre><code>Reconciliation Period: Month YYYY
Number of Accounts: X
Total Recharge: $XXX
Total Spend: $XXX
Difference: $XXX (X%)
Difference Reason: Pending/Confirmed
Invoice Status: Received/Pending
Notes: [Exception details]
</code></pre>
<h3>Archiving Requirements</h3>
<p>Archive the report alongside original documents (recharge receipts, spend reports, invoice PDFs) in cloud storage. Use this naming convention: "YYYYMM_Platform_ReconciliationReport" for easy retrieval during financial audits.</p>
<hr />
<h2>FAQ</h2>
<p><strong>Q: What if the credited amount differs from the charged amount?</strong></p>
<p>Verify whether platform service fees or bank transfer fees were deducted. Keep payment receipts and contact platform support if needed.</p>
<p><strong>Q: Is it normal for the invoice amount to be lower than actual spend?</strong></p>
<p>Yes, in most cases. Some platforms issue invoices showing only "consumed amount," not the current account balance. If the difference is significant, confirm there are no unrecorded refunds.</p>
<p><strong>Q: How do I handle cross-month discrepancies?</strong></p>
<p>Use "invoice issuance date" as the baseline to attribute recharge and spend to the corresponding month. This prevents cross-month differences from distorting monthly reports.</p>
